## Layer 3: Hardened VFS Architecture GROQ Verification

### Scope Contract
Fix the `param $leafId referenced, but not provided` and syntax errors in `verify-mapping.mjs`. Implement proper array-intersection GROQ and decoupled Sub-Tree logic.

### DoD

- [ ] 3.1 — In `scripts/product-vfs-mapper/verify-mapping.mjs`: Fix the **Positive Leaf Test**. The target GROQ query must be valid Sanity array-membership syntax: `*[_type == "product" && $leafId in catalogueLocationKeys]{name}`.
- [ ] 3.2 — Fix the **Positive Header (Sub-Tree) Test**. GROQ does not easily recursively query unbounded parent-child trees. Instead:
  1. Write a Javascript function `getSubTreeLeafIds(headerId)` that queries `*[_type == "catalogueItem"]` and recursively follows `parent._ref` downwards in-memory to collect all leaf node IDs.
  2. Perform the product intersection query seamlessly: `*[_type == "product" && count((catalogueLocationKeys[])[@ in $subTreeIds]) > 0]{name}`.
- [ ] 3.3 — Fix the **Negative Co-Branch Test**. Use the same valid membership syntax `$leafId in catalogueLocationKeys` to assert the product does NOT match logically contradictory siblings.
- [ ] 3.4 — Prevent `Cannot read properties of null` by gracefully testing ONLY against the leaf IDs strictly present inside the database right now (dynamic querying instead of hardcoded strings).

### Verification — Layer 3

- [ ] V3.1 — Run `node scripts/product-vfs-mapper/verify-mapping.mjs --productId="[Target_ID]"`.
- [ ] V3.2 — Look for 100% green-checks spanning Positive Leaf, Postive Header, and Negative Co-Branch tests. The GROQ parsing errors must not appear. Test is PASS.

